    "gold_solution": "```python\nfrom gurobipy import *\n\ndef solve_problem():\n    m = Model(\"JSSP\")\n    m.setParam('TimeLimit', 300)\n\n    # --- Data ---\n    n_jobs = 5\n    n_machines = 3\n    processing_times = [[19, 25, 29], [27, 11, 25], [24, 13, 6], [22, 16, 25], [24, 15, 26]]\n    machine_assignments = [[0, 1, 2], [2, 1, 0], [1, 0, 2], [2, 0, 1], [2, 0, 1]]\n\n    # Big-M: sum of all processing times\n    M = sum(processing_times[j][o] for j in range(n_jobs) for o in range(n_machines))\n\n    # --- Variables ---\n    # Start times for each operation\n    S = {}\n    for j in range(n_jobs):\n        for o in range(n_machines):\n            S[(j,o)] = m.addVar(lb=0, ub=M, vtype=GRB.CONTINUOUS, name=f\"Start_{j}_{o}\")\n\n    # Makespan variable\n    Cmax = m.addVar(lb=0, ub=M, vtype=GRB.CONTINUOUS, name=\"Cmax\")\n\n    # Build machine -> [(job, op)] mapping\n    machine_ops = {mach: [] for mach in range(n_machines)}\n    for j in range(n_jobs):\n        for o in range(n_machines):\n            machine_ops[machine_assignments[j][o]].append((j, o))\n\n    # Disjunctive binary variables for each pair on same machine\n    Y = {}\n    for mach in range(n_machines):\n        ops = machine_ops[mach]\n        for i in range(len(ops)):\n            for k in range(i+1, len(ops)):\n                j1, o1 = ops[i]\n                j2, o2 = ops[k]\n                Y[(j1,o1,j2,o2)] = m.addVar(vtype=GRB.BINARY, name=f\"Y_{j1}_{o1}_{j2}_{o2}\")\n\n    # --- Objective: minimize makespan ---\n    m.setObjective(Cmax, GRB.MINIMIZE)\n\n    # --- Constraints ---\n    # 1. Precedence: operations within a job must follow order\n    for j in range(n_jobs):\n        for o in range(n_machines - 1):\n            m.addConstr(\n                S[(j,o+1)] >= S[(j,o)] + processing_times[j][o],\n                name=f\"Prec_{j}_{o}\")\n\n    # 2. Disjunctive: no two operations on the same machine overlap\n    for mach in range(n_machines):\n        ops = machine_ops[mach]\n        for i in range(len(ops)):\n            for k in range(i+1, len(ops)):\n                j1, o1 = ops[i]\n                j2, o2 = ops[k]\n                p1 = processing_times[j1][o1]\n                p2 = processing_times[j2][o2]\n                y = Y[(j1,o1,j2,o2)]\n                m.addConstr(\n                    S[(j1,o1)] + p1 <= S[(j2,o2)] + M * (1 - y),\n                    name=f\"Disj_A_{j1}_{o1}_{j2}_{o2}\")\n                m.addConstr(\n                    S[(j2,o2)] + p2 <= S[(j1,o1)] + M * y,\n                    name=f\"Disj_B_{j1}_{o1}_{j2}_{o2}\")\n\n    # 3. Makespan: Cmax >= completion of last operation of each job\n    for j in range(n_jobs):\n        last_op = n_machines - 1\n        m.addConstr(\n            Cmax >= S[(j, last_op)] + processing_times[j][last_op],\n            name=f\"Makespan_J{j}\")\n\n    m.optimize()\n    return m\n```",
    "LLM_description": "Subject: Coordinated Production Scheduling in Our Job Shop\n\nContext\nOur facility operates as a job shop with multiple specialized workstations. Current demand includes several distinct customer orders that each require movement through a defined set of operations across these machines. Each operation has a known duration and must be performed on a designated workstation. Processing times vary across tasks and machines, with some steps being short and others considerably longer.\n\nScheduling Challenge\nBecause different jobs compete for the same machines and each job\u2019s steps must occur in a set order, we need a coordinated schedule that determines when each operation should start and finish on each workstation. The challenge is to sequence all work so that machines are well utilized, waiting time between steps is minimized, and bottlenecks are avoided, while still respecting the constraints below.\n\nKey Operating Requirements\n- Fixed sequences by job: The operations for a given job must be completed in the prescribed order; a step cannot start until the prior step in that job is finished.\n- Machine specificity: Each operation must run on its required workstation; substitutions are not permitted.\n- One job at a time per machine: A workstation can process only one operation at any moment.\n- Continuity of processing: Once an operation starts, it runs to completion without interruption or split processing.\n- Known durations: Operation times are known in advance and must be incorporated into the plan.\n\nGoal\nBuild a feasible, conflict-free schedule that completes all jobs in the shortest possible overall time (makespan). Success will be measured by how quickly all work is finished while honoring the rules above and limiting idle time and work-in-process queues.\n\nNext Steps\nPlanning will consolidate the required sequences and durations, test alternative machine sequences to relieve likely bottlenecks, and publish a detailed dispatch plan with start/finish times for each operation. We will review the proposed schedule with Production and Maintenance to confirm feasibility and align on execution.\n\nANNEX \u2013 Production Data\n- Total number of jobs: 5\n- Total number of machines (workstations): 3\n- Machine assignments by job and operation sequence: Machine Assignments (which machine each operation uses):\n  Machine indices: 0 to 2\n  - Job 1: [Op1: Machine 0, Op2: Machine 1, Op3: Machine 2]\n  - Job 2: [Op1: Machine 2, Op2: Machine 1, Op3: Machine 0]\n  - Job 3: [Op1: Machine 1, Op2: Machine 0, Op3: Machine 2]\n  - Job 4: [Op1: Machine 2, Op2: Machine 0, Op3: Machine 1]\n  - Job 5: [Op1: Machine 2, Op2: Machine 0, Op3: Machine 1]\n- Operation processing times (time units): Processing Times (time units per operation):\n  Each job has 3 operations performed in sequence.\n  - Job 1: [Op1: 19, Op2: 25, Op3: 29]\n  - Job 2: [Op1: 27, Op2: 11, Op3: 25]\n  - Job 3: [Op1: 24, Op2: 13, Op3: 6]\n  - Job 4: [Op1: 22, Op2: 16, Op3: 25]\n  - Job 5: [Op1: 24, Op2: 15, Op3: 26]",
    "LLM_description_template": "Subject: Coordinated Production Scheduling in Our Job Shop\n\nContext\nOur facility operates as a job shop with multiple specialized workstations. Current demand includes several distinct customer orders that each require movement through a defined set of operations across these machines. Each operation has a known duration and must be performed on a designated workstation. Processing times vary across tasks and machines, with some steps being short and others considerably longer.\n\nScheduling Challenge\nBecause different jobs compete for the same machines and each job\u2019s steps must occur in a set order, we need a coordinated schedule that determines when each operation should start and finish on each workstation. The challenge is to sequence all work so that machines are well utilized, waiting time between steps is minimized, and bottlenecks are avoided, while still respecting the constraints below.\n\nKey Operating Requirements\n- Fixed sequences by job: The operations for a given job must be completed in the prescribed order; a step cannot start until the prior step in that job is finished.\n- Machine specificity: Each operation must run on its required workstation; substitutions are not permitted.\n- One job at a time per machine: A workstation can process only one operation at any moment.\n- Continuity of processing: Once an operation starts, it runs to completion without interruption or split processing.\n- Known durations: Operation times are known in advance and must be incorporated into the plan.\n\nGoal\nBuild a feasible, conflict-free schedule that completes all jobs in the shortest possible overall time (makespan). Success will be measured by how quickly all work is finished while honoring the rules above and limiting idle time and work-in-process queues.\n\nNext Steps\nPlanning will consolidate the required sequences and durations, test alternative machine sequences to relieve likely bottlenecks, and publish a detailed dispatch plan with start/finish times for each operation. We will review the proposed schedule with Production and Maintenance to confirm feasibility and align on execution.\n\nANNEX \u2013 Production Data\n- Total number of jobs: {NUM_JOBS}\n- Total number of machines (workstations): {NUM_MACHINES}\n- Machine assignments by job and operation sequence: {MACHINE_ASSIGNMENTS}\n- Operation processing times (time units): {PROCESSING_TIMES}"
